---
// Where a table invitation points: `/i/CODE`.
//
// This is the home page's document with a different <head>, and that is the
// entire feature. A link dropped in a chat window unfurls into whatever the
// *served* HTML says — crawlers and unfurlers run no JavaScript — so an
// invitation could only say "somebody kept you a seat" by being a document of
// its own. Everything below the head is `GamePage`, unchanged: same `#root`,
// same bundled entry, same game.
//
// **One built page answers every code.** Astro emits `/i/index.html` and nothing
// else; `/i/ABC234` reaches it through a fallback scoped to this directory, in
// `client/nginx.conf` for production and in `astro.config.mjs` for `astro dev`,
// the preview server and Playwright. A static build cannot emit a page per
// table, and the site's own rule — a miss is a real 404, never the app
// (`nginx.conf`) — is left standing everywhere outside `/i/`.
//
// `chrome={false}`: the footer row, the sheet and the burger are the indexable
// half of `/`, and this page is `noindex`. Somebody arriving here was handed a
// seat, not sent five links and a page of prose.
//
// `langNeutral`: an invitation carries no language (`hooks/tableInvite.ts`), so
// there is no `data-served-lang` for `initLang` to act on and the reader's own
// browser decides, exactly as it does at `/`. Nothing here is swapped either:
// `chrome={false}` leaves this document with no served copy in it at all.
import GamePage from '../../layouts/GamePage.astro'
import { INVITE } from '../../seo/meta'
---

<GamePage lang="en" page={INVITE} jsonLd={null} noindex langNeutral chrome={false} />
